2 CONT <p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: SourceSansPro-Regular; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; col
2 CONC or: #36322d;">1867- Emigrated from Bergen to Quebec with his parents and brother Ingvard on 23 April 1867 on the ship Claus Heftye. Listed as Halvard Ovrevold age 24 bornin Laerdal.&nbsp; </span></p>imes New Roman'; mso-bidi-font-
2 CONC family: 'Times New Roman'; color: #36322d;">1872- Witnesses to the marriage were Peter Hoidal and Hakon Pedersen.</span></p>0001pt; line-height: normal;"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: SourceSansPro-Regular; mso-fare
2 CONC ast-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: #36322d;">1880- Riceland Township, Freeborn County, Minnesota Census family listed as Halver Ingvardson age 37, Anna age 27 both born in Norway, L
2 CONC eina age 7, Mary age 4, and Nettie age 2 all born in Minnesota.&nbsp;</span></p>'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: #36322d;">27 Oct 1881 - Halvor Ingvaldson was the plaintiff vs. D.M. Osborn, the case went on until
2 CONC  25 May 1882 and he won a verdict of $150.00.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p>Roman'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; color: #36322d;">Obituary - Old Man Dies Suddenly Albert Lea Standard 23 Sep 1905.Halvor Ingvaldson died suddenly
2 CONC  at the home of his son Edward in Riceland Tuesday evening of a stroke of apoplexy. He had attended the county fair and left here for the farm about six o'clock in the evening. After arriving he and his son began milking. Suddenly
2 CONC , his son heard his father's milk pail overturn, and looking around saw his father prostrate on the ground. He was taken to the house and died about midnight. The funeral will be held this afternoon from theEast Church in Bancroft
2 CONC . The interment will be in the cemetery by the church. Rev. H.J. Strand will officiate. Mr. Ingvaldson was born in Sogn. Norway about 65 years ago. He came to this country about 1872 (really in 1867 Tom Cotton) and settled on his 
2 CONC farm in Riceland. About four years ago he moved to this city to reside. He has been helping his son on his farm this summer. He was held in high esteem and respect by all who knew him. He leaves his wife and six children to mourn 
2 CONC his death. Mrs. N.G. Bagaasen, Mrs. W. J. Anderson, both of Bancroft, Miss Nettie, who resided on the home farm withher brother and his family. Mrs. Louis Kaasa, Mrs. John Christensen and Miss Alma, of this city, and Edward, at th
2 CONC e home place.</span></em></p>
